Here is a clean template for an academic article using the standard `article` class. Save it as `main.tex` and compile with `pdflatex` (and `bibtex` if you use a bibliography).

```latex
\documentclass[12pt, a4paper]{article}

% ---------- Packages ----------
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{setspace}
\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}
\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}

\onehalfspacing

% ---------- Title ----------
\title{Your Article Title Goes Here}
\author{
  First Author\thanks{Department, University. Email: first@example.edu} \and
  Second Author\thanks{Department, University. Email: second@example.edu}
}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Write a concise summary of your research question, methods, main results,
and conclusions in roughly 150 to 250 words.

\bigskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} keyword one, keyword two, keyword three
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Introduce the topic, explain why it matters, review the key literature
briefly, and state your research question or hypothesis \citep{example2020}.

\section{Methods}
Describe your data, materials, and procedures in enough detail for
someone else to reproduce your work.

\subsection{Data}
Describe your data sources here.

\section{Results}
Present your findings. Equations can be written like this:
\begin{equation}
  y = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x + \varepsilon
  \label{eq:model}
\end{equation}
and referenced as Equation~\ref{eq:model}.

\begin{table}[h]
  \centering
  \caption{Example table.}
  \label{tab:example}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Group & Mean & SD \\
    \midrule
    A & 3.2 & 0.5 \\
    B & 4.1 & 0.7 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  % \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{figure.png}
  \caption{Example figure caption.}
  \label{fig:example}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion}
Interpret your results, discuss limitations, and compare with prior work.

\section{Conclusion}
Summarize the main contributions and suggest future work.

\section*{Acknowledgments}
Thank funders and contributors here.

\bibliographystyle{plainnat}
\bibliography{references}

\end{document}
```

You will also need a `references.bib` file, for example:

```bibtex
@article{example2020,
  author  = {Smith, John and Lee, Ann},
  title   = {An Example Paper},
  journal = {Journal of Examples},
  year    = {2020},
  volume  = {12},
  pages   = {1--20}
}
```

Compile in this order: `pdflatex main`, `bibtex main`, `pdflatex main`, `pdflatex main`. If your instructor or journal supplies its own class file (such as `IEEEtran` or `elsarticle`), swap out the `\documentclass` line and follow their formatting guidelines.
